{
  "extends": "../../tsconfig.base.json",
  "compilerOptions": {
    "rootDir": "src",
    "outDir": "dist",
    "tsBuildInfoFile": "dist/.tsbuildinfo",
    /**
     * **No ambient types at all**, and `lib` is `ES2023` with no DOM (see
     * `tsconfig.base.json`). This package is imported by the browser app, by
     * the API and workers under node, and by `@flux/mocks` — as *source*, not
     * as built declarations — so it must typecheck under the intersection of
     * all three environments, which is the bare language.
     *
     * This used to be `types: ["node"]`, justified as "node only, never the
     * DOM". Half of that was enforced by `lib`; the other half was backwards.
     * Node types are what a browser consumer does not have, so the config
     * permitted exactly the import that breaks the bundle — a schema file
     * reaching for `process.env` or `Buffer` would have compiled here and
     * failed there. It also broke `@flux/mocks`, whose own `types: []` is the
     * same guarantee stated deliberately: `crypto.getRandomValues` in
     * `ids.ts` resolved under this setting and nowhere else.
     *
     * The one platform API this package genuinely needs is declared at its
     * call site instead. Test files get node back via `tsconfig.test.json`,
     * where it is true.
     */
    "types": []
  },
  /**
   * Build config: tests are excluded so they never end up in dist/ or in the
   * emitted .d.ts. They are still typechecked — see tsconfig.test.json,
   * which is what `pnpm typecheck` runs.
   */
  "include": ["src/**/*.ts"],
  "exclude": ["src/**/*.test.ts", "dist", "node_modules"]
}
